# VISION.md

# Project Codex Vision

*"Software should help people think—not simply consume."*

---

# Why We Exist

The modern internet has optimized reading for speed.

Summaries replace understanding.

Algorithms replace curiosity.

Infinite feeds replace sustained attention.

Artificial intelligence increasingly encourages people to read less, think less, and produce more.

We believe this is backwards.

Reading is not merely information transfer.

Reading is how humans construct knowledge, develop wisdom, generate creativity, and understand one another.

Project Codex exists to restore reading as an act of thinking.

---

# Our Mission

Build the world's best environment for deep reading, research, and intellectual exploration.

Not another ebook reader.

Not another chatbot.

Not another productivity app.

A place where ideas can grow.

---

# Our Philosophy

Technology should amplify human intelligence.

Not replace it.

AI should make us better thinkers.

Not faster consumers.

Books should become conversations.

Research should become exploration.

Notes should become living knowledge.

---

# Our Principles

## Reading Comes First

Every feature must improve reading.

If a feature distracts from reading, it does not belong.

---

## Calm Over Productivity

We reject software designed around urgency.

No feeds.

No engagement metrics.

No notifications demanding attention.

The product should feel like entering a quiet library.

---

## AI Is An Assistant

AI is never the author.

AI is never the center of attention.

AI waits to be invited.

Its purpose is to ask thoughtful questions, reveal connections, challenge assumptions, and expand understanding.

The reader always remains the thinker.

---

## Thinking Is Nonlinear

Ideas emerge gradually.

Knowledge is connected.

Books connect to papers.

Papers connect to notes.

Notes connect to conversations.

Conversations connect to entirely new ideas.

The software should help users discover these relationships naturally.

---

## Preserve Intellectual Ownership

Everything belongs to the reader.

Highlights.

Annotations.

Conversations.

Knowledge graphs.

Research.

Users should always be able to export their work.

No lock-in.

No proprietary formats.

Knowledge should remain portable.

---

## Beauty Matters

Beautiful software changes behavior.

Typography matters.

Whitespace matters.

Motion matters.

Silence matters.

A reading environment should feel crafted rather than engineered.

Every interface should reduce cognitive load rather than increase it.

---

# What We Believe

We believe reading on screens can feel as satisfying as reading on paper.

We believe AI can deepen scholarship rather than dilute it.

We believe note-taking should generate ideas rather than archives.

We believe research should feel like exploration.

We believe software can encourage contemplation.

We believe the best technology often disappears into the background.

---

# Our Long-Term Vision

Create the operating system for intellectual life.

One place where people can:

Read.

Think.

Question.

Annotate.

Research.

Write.

Connect ideas.

Remember.

Create.

Whether reading a novel, an academic paper, a government report, or a historical manuscript, every interaction contributes to a lifelong, interconnected body of knowledge.

Project Codex becomes a trusted companion throughout a person's intellectual journey.

---

# What We Will Never Become

We will never optimize for addiction.

We will never build infinite scrolling feeds.

We will never prioritize engagement over understanding.

We will never encourage AI-generated knowledge in place of genuine learning.

We will never reduce books to summaries.

We will never measure success by time spent inside the application.

Our success is measured by the quality of thinking that happens after users close it.

---

# Our North Star

Every decision should answer one question:

**Does this help someone think more deeply?**

If the answer is no, we do not build it.

If the answer is yes, we build it beautifully.

---

*"Great books change people.*

*Great software should help them do it."*
